<!-- Exercise 063 - StyleSetterAndTransition (intermediate).
     Goal:   Drive one named Border entirely from a Style: a plain Setter for its
     Background, and a Setter for Transitions carrying a DoubleTransition on
     Opacity. Leave the sibling Border untouched, so the selector has to be
     scoped rather than hitting every Border in the view.
     Drills: Style Selector by name, Setter, the Transitions property as a Style
     Setter, DoubleTransition, and the fact that a transition DEFERS a value
     instead of applying it at once.
     Passes: dotnet test, filtered to Ex063_ (see
     Ex063_StyleSetterAndTransition.axaml.cs for the exact command, because an
     XML comment may not hold two hyphens in a row).

     WHAT THIS EXERCISE DOES NOT ASSERT, AND WHY: animation progress. In this
     headless harness nothing pumps the animation clock, and there is no public
     way to pump it either - IClock, ClockBase and Animatable.Clock are all
     internal in Avalonia 12.1.1. Measured: a 200 ms transition moved 0.010 across
     505 forced render-timer ticks, which was just the 2 ms of real time those
     ticks took. So a test that waits for Opacity to reach its target would be a
     wall-clock race. What IS deterministic is that the transition is attached and
     that it holds the old value at the instant the new one is assigned, which is
     exactly what the test below checks. The long 5 second duration is chosen so
     that a few milliseconds of real time cannot blur that reading. -->
<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Intermediate.Ex063_StyleSetterAndTransition">
  <!-- TODO: replace the placeholder below with two Borders and a Style that
       matches only the first of them:

         <StackPanel Spacing="8">
           <StackPanel.Styles>
             <Style Selector="Border#Fader">
               <Setter Property="Background" Value="#FF3366" />
               <Setter Property="Transitions">
                 <Transitions>
                   <DoubleTransition Property="Opacity" Duration="0:0:5" />
                 </Transitions>
               </Setter>
             </Style>
           </StackPanel.Styles>
           <Border Name="Fader" Width="40" Height="40" />
           <Border Name="Instant" Width="40" Height="40" Background="#CCCCCC" />
         </StackPanel>

       Two things the test pins down, so shortcuts do not score. First, the
       Background and the Transitions must arrive at STYLE priority: setting
       Background="#FF3366" directly on the Border, or nesting a
       <Border.Transitions> element inside it, produces the same rendered result
       at LocalValue priority and is rejected. Second, Instant must keep no
       transitions at all, so a selector of plain "Border" - which would sweep up
       both - is rejected as well. -->
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Text="TODO" />
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
